// Preload: what the language itself refers to, written in Rig. Every
// program imports it without an #import (spec 2.14): an ordinary program,
// a build program and each of its workspaces, rig check, and the world of
// #run while compiling. rig finds it where it finds every module that ships
// with it, and reads this file right after the program's main file. In
// load order it comes first, before the main file (spec 2.13), so its
// declarations come first: a program that declares a name Preload declares
// is told so at its own declaration. Writing
//
//     #import "Preload";
//
// is allowed and reads nothing more, as a #load of this file by its path
// doesn't: a file is read once.
//
// What Preload declares joins the program's one top level, so a program
// can't declare those names. README.md lists the ones that are for
// programs. Every name Preload declares for its own use starts with two
// underscores (__malloc, say), which leaves the plain names to programs:
// a convention, which the compiler doesn't enforce.
